  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cool high pressure will gradually diminish \nthrough the remainder of the weekend. \n\nHigh pressure remains situated across the lower Mid-Atlantic \nthis morning, with northeasterly winds drawing cool maritime air\nfrom the New England area. Radar imagery indicates pockets of \nlight rain drifting east across the region as a weak disturbance\nexits toward the Atlantic coast. Further to the west, a large \ndome of high pressure continues to build over the Four Corners \nregion of the southwestern United States. \n\nFor today, showers will decrease through the morning, with \nbreaks of sunshine developing during the afternoon as the flow \nof cool maritime air begins to weaken. Regardless, the cool air \nwill still hold for one more day, with highs ranging from the \nlow 70s to the low 80s. This will help to strictly limit the \ndevelopment of any showers or storms for today, and no hazardous\nweather is forecasted.\n\nThe dome of high pressure across the Four Corners region will \ncontinue to build Sunday, causing windflow aloft to switch from \nthe northwest. Will be watching for upper level disturbances \nriding around the dome to pass across the lower Mid-Atlantic, \npotentially triggering rounds of shower and thunderstorm \nactivity. Latest weather forecast data hints at one such \ndisturbance as early as Sunday evening across the mountains, \nthough confidence is currently low as to when or where any \nassociated thunderstorm activity will develop, if at all. \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Thunderstorm potential will increase during the\nearly half of the workweek another cold front approaches.\n\nConfidence is increasing that a disturbance caught within the \nnorthwest wind flow aloft will bring another round of energy \nduring late Monday into Tuesday, with a strong cold front \nmoving through sometime late Tuesday into early Wednesday. This \nis forecasted to trigger a round of organized strong to severe \nthunderstorms capable of both damaging winds and heavy rain that\nmay result in localized flash flooding. The lower Mid-Atlantic \nis outlooked for both a 15% chance of severe thunderstorms for \nTuesday, as well as a Marginal Risk of Excessive Rainfall. \n\nThere appears to be timing differences within the latest data, \nwith some data suggesting that strong thunderstorms could arrive\nfrom the north late Monday into early Tuesday morning before \nfading. Thunderstorms may then redevelop during Tuesday \nafternoon and evening somewhere across our area, introducing\nanother severe threat. We will continue to monitor this threat \nas it evolves.",
